Now I see the issue. In `page_for_insert`:

```c
inline page_t *page_for_insert (hb_codepoint_t g)
{
    page_map_t map = {get_major (g), pages.len};
    unsigned int i;
    if (!page_map.bfind (map, &i))
    {
      if (!resize (pages.len + 1))
        return nullptr;

      pages[map.index].init0 ();
      memmove (&page_map[i + 1], &page_map[i], (page_map.len - 1 - i) * sizeof (page_map[0]));
      page_map[i] = map;
    }
    return &pages[page_map[i].index];
}
```

The `bfind` function uses `this->arrayZ[mid].cmp (&x)`. The `arrayZ` is the `page_map` array. If `page_map.len` is 0 (empty set), then `max = -1`, and the while loop doesn't execute. Then `if (max < 0 || ...)` → `max++` → `max = 0`. Then `*i = 0`. So `i = 0`. Then `page_map[i] = map` writes to index 0. But wait, `page_map.len` is 0, so `page_map[0]` is out of bounds... unless `resize` was called.

Actually, wait. Let me re-read. In `page_for_insert`, `bfind` is called. If `page_map.len == 0`, then `max = -1`, the while loop doesn't run, `max < 0` is true, so `max++` → `max = 0`, `*i = 0`. Returns false. Then `resize(pages.len + 1)` is called. Then `pages[map.index].init0()`. Then `memmove(&page_map[i+1], &page_map[i], (page_map.len - 1 - i) * sizeof(page_map[0]))`. With `i=0` and `page_map.len=0`, this is `memmove(&page_map[1], &page_map[0], (0-1-0) * sizeof(...))` = `memmove(..., ..., -1 * sizeof(...))`. That's a huge size_t (underflow). Hmm, but that's a different bug.

